# CIS IG1 audit — settings
#
# One file per engagement. Copy it, fill it in, and no command needs a path
# or an ID again:
#
#   cp config/audit.env.example config/audit.env
#
# config/audit.env is gitignored. It holds the customer's answers — their
# project naming, their registries, their data-residency policy — which are
# theirs to publish, not yours. Never put a credential in here: the audit
# runs by impersonation precisely so that no key exists.
#
# These are policy answers, not facts about the estate. Ask the customer
# rather than inferring them from what happens to be deployed. If a resource
# genuinely does not exist, set the value to
#   none
# and the checks that need it are recorded as FINDINGS, not skipped — a
# resource that does not exist cannot meet the requirement.

# --- The organization -------------------------------------------------
# Set these once. run-audit.sh reads ORG_ID; the setup steps in
# docs/cis-ig1-auditor-setup.md read all three.

# The organization to audit — the numeric ID, not the domain:
#   gcloud organizations list
ORG_ID=123456789012

# The project that owns the audit service account and has the audit APIs
# enabled. Used by the setup steps, not by the audit itself.
AUDIT_PROJECT=acme-audit

# The service account the auditor impersonates. Used by the setup steps,
# not by the audit itself.
SA_EMAIL=cis-ig1-auditor@acme-audit.iam.gserviceaccount.com

# Your branch for this engagement, named after you so a run is attributable.
# Set it per auditor; do not derive it from `whoami`, which is a property of
# the machine and silently creates a second branch on a second laptop.
AUDIT_BRANCH=dana/cis-ig1-audit

# --- Check inputs ------------------------------------------------------

# Approved container registry prefixes, comma-separated. Matched from the
# left of the image reference, so a prefix covers everything beneath it.
# Two checks read this (V20, V22); leaving it empty makes both SKIP, which
# holds the audit below 100% complete. If the customer has no approved list,
# set it to  none  — that is a finding against safeguard 2.3, not missing
# data.
APPROVED_REGISTRIES=us-docker.pkg.dev/acme,gcr.io/acme,gke.gcr.io

# Projects never audited — a regular expression matched against the project
# ID. ^sys- skips the projects Apps Script creates. Set to  none  to audit
# everything.
EXCLUDE_PROJECTS=^sys-

# Locations data may legitimately live in, comma-separated. Defaults to the
# continental United States, so leave it commented out unless data lives
# elsewhere. Setting it REPLACES the default list rather than extending it,
# so give the full set — a short list turns every other US region into a
# finding.
# ALLOWED_LOCATIONS=us,US,us-central1,us-east1,us-east4,us-east5,us-south1,us-west1,us-west2,us-west3,us-west4
